everal drug classes that either increase hyperkalemia risk or exacerbate gastrointestinal toxicity. Chloride potassium interacts with adrenocortical glucocorticoids—especially those with mineralocorticoid activity—which may influence potassium excretion [Data: Relationships (41549)]. Potassium citrate, meanwhile, shows problematic interactions with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory analgesics (NSAIDs), which worsen its gastrointestinal side effects [Data: Relationships (39678)], and with drugs that delay gastric emptying (e.g., anticholinergics), which prolong contact time between potassium salts and the gut mucosa, intensifying irritation [Data: Relationships (39669)]. Additionally, mineralocorticoids reduce the efficacy of potassium salts by promoting urinary potassium loss [Data: Relationships (39674)]. These interactions illustrate a complex web of pharmacodynamic and pharmacokinetic influences affecting both safety and therapeutic outcomes.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Stored blood contributes to hyperkalemia risk in potassium therapy\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Stored blood (库存血) is identified as a significant source of exogenous potassium, containing 30 mmol/L if stored fewer than 10 days and up to 65 mmol/L if stored longer. When administered alongside potassium-containing drugs like potassium citrate or chloride potassium—or with potassium-sparing agents such as spironolactone—it substantially increases the likelihood of hyperkalemia, particularly in patients with preexisting renal impairment [Data: Relationships (39668)]. This highlights a critical, often overlooked iatrogenic source of potassium overload in clinical settings involving transfusions and electrolyte supplementation.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"The community reflects a high-risk pharmacovigilance network centered on electrolyte management\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The entities and relationships form a tightly interconnected clinica